# Guardian AI — Frontend Rules

- Use React, Vite, TypeScript, and Tailwind CSS — the stack is already decided, do not
  introduce alternatives.
- Never use the `any` type. Use precise types/interfaces for all props, state, and API
  data.
- Keep business logic and severity/status/decision calculations in the backend. The
  frontend only renders what the API returns — never recompute or override severity,
  classification, or the final decision client-side.
- Use typed API response models (mirrored or generated from the backend schemas) for all
  data coming from the API client.
- Keep components small and presentational; extract a component if it grows beyond one
  clear rendering responsibility.
- Avoid unnecessary state-management libraries (Redux, Zustand, etc.) — local component
  state and props are sufficient for this scope.
- Avoid Next.js-specific patterns (file-based routing, SSR/API routes, `getServerSideProps`,
  etc.) — this is a Vite SPA.
- Keep the UI focused on the single review workflow (submit review → view result). Do not
  add extra screens, navigation, or history views.
- Provide accessible labels (e.g. `aria-label`, associated `<label>` elements) and visible
  loading/error states for every user-triggered action.
- Avoid adding features outside the documented MVP (see `docs/ROADMAP.md`); if unsure
  whether something is in scope, check the roadmap before building it.
